# ViewSonic Commercial Display (LFD RS-232 & LAN protocol)
# Reference: ViewSonic "LFD RS-232 & LAN Protocol Specification" rev 3.3.2
#            (Dec 2020), the brand-wide grammar every CDE/ViewBoard/NMP model
#            publishes per model at manuals.viewsonic.com. The CDE31 series
#            user guide (viewsonicglobal.com, CDE31_Series_UG_ENG.pdf, June
#            2026) prints the same Set table up to rev 3.2.6 and no Get table.
# Protocol: ASCII framed packets, CR terminated, over TCP 5000 (no login) or
#           RS-232 9600 8N1 (fixed).
#
# Verified on a CDE8631-1C (firmware 20250609234259, then 20260526125505).
# What that unit does beyond the document, and what the driver does about it:
#   - Power: Set Power on from standby takes about 3 s and keeps the
#     connection. Standby depends on the Advanced menu's Power Save: Sleep
#     keeps the network and answers everything; Hibernate drops off the
#     network about 6 s after Power Off. A magic packet woke it in neither
#     mode with WOL on. While a system update is waiting, Power Off brings up
#     the update prompt on screen and the display stays on until someone
#     answers it.
#   - Changes made with the IR remote (volume, mute, input, power) are
#     pushed like the document says.
#   - Several TCP connections are served at once, and every one of them
#     receives the auto-reply frames, including after a change made over LAN
#     (the spec describes them for changes made by a user).
#   - The display looks for changes once a second and pushes what changed,
#     so a push lands up to about a second after the ack; an input change is
#     pushed about 5 s after it takes effect, and also pushes brightness.
#   - Contrast, sharpness, color, tint and the tiling values are not pushed.
#   - Rarely (twice in about sixty tries) a command sent within a second of
#     another level or mute change was acked and not applied.
#   So every set whose value a get reads correctly straight after the ack
#     carries that get in the same send (source, volume, mute, brightness,
#     the picture values, screen blank, tiling), and the state follows the
#     display at once, a dropped command included.
#   - Volume Up / Down and freeze apply after their ack, so a get sent with
#     them reads the old value; they send no read-back and the push brings
#     the new one (freeze in the Function On_Off form, r=102 / r=002).
#   - Screen Unblank holds its ack about 2 s until the backlight is lit. Sent
#     within about 2 s of a Screen Blank it is acked at once and ignored;
#     its read-back then reports the screen still blank.
#   - Info replies are padded with spaces instead of NULs (the runtime strips
#     trailing whitespace; the rules accept both).
#   - The ambient sensor get takes about 2 s and holds every reply behind
#     it, so it is polled only when the ambient sensor option is on. The
#     CDE8631-1C answers it without a Smart Hub accessory, from its own
#     temperature and light sensors (humidity 0, presence off). The fields
#     are not fixed-width (A28.2B000.0C-1D00000), and each single-field get
#     answers "none" on a unit without the accessory.
#   - While the embedded Android source is showing, brightness, contrast and
#     sharpness sets are refused (and the sharpness get); Backlight sets the
#     level there. Color and tint still apply.
#   - PIP, surround and the customized hot key are refused, and the slot-in
#     PC input is refused with no OPS module installed.
#   - Tiling position reports the stored position with tiling off.
#
#   Set:    [len][ID two digits]['s'][code][value 3 bytes]<CR>
#   Get:    [len][ID two digits]['g'][code]['000']<CR>
#   Ack:    [len][ID]['+' or '-']<CR>                (set result)
#   Reply:  [len][ID]['r'][code][value...]<CR>       (get answer)
#   IR:     [len][ID]['p'][key MSB][key LSB]<CR>     (RCU pass-through)
#
# The length byte is the ASCII digit of the byte count excluding CR: '8' for
# the standard 9-byte packet, which is every command here, so it is part of
# command_prefix with the Monitor ID. Info replies (device name, MAC, IP,
# serial, firmware, operation hours, smart hub) use the fixed "32-byte
# format": the value is padded (NULs per the spec, spaces on the CDE31) and
# its length byte is a literal '2', so every response rule matches
# positionally and strips the padding itself.
#
# The backlight level rides its own command-type pair inherited from the
# color-calibration command set: set type 'A' / get type 'a', code 'B'.
# Monitor ID is 01 for LAN control; RS-232 daisy chains address IDs 1-98, so
# the ID is a config field and replies for other IDs match no rule.
#
# Push vs poll: HYBRID. Since protocol 3.2.1 the display auto-replies (sends
# the updated power / input / brightness / backlight / volume / mute frame
# unsolicited when one changes); the CDE31 also pushes backlight on/off and
# freeze in the Function On_Off form. The rules below match any 'r' frame
# whenever it arrives, and polling refreshes the rest.
#
# Power on: "001" on, "000" standby. Whether LAN control survives standby
# depends on the display's standby/network setting; the spec says power-on
# via LAN "may work only under specific modes" and offers "WOL by MAC" as
# the alternative. Power On is the protocol's own Set Power 001, the one
# that worked on hardware; Wake (Wake-on-LAN) sends the magic packet to
# the MAC learned on every connection (or typed in), declared
# available_offline so it runs while the display is unreachable. 2.0.0
# made the magic packet Power On; a CDE8631-1C ignored it in every mode,
# so 2.1.0 swapped them.
#
# 2.0.0 is the conversion from the 1.x Python driver; every command, state
# variable and device setting kept its name and tokens.

id: viewsonic_cde
name: ViewSonic Commercial Display
manufacturer: ViewSonic
category: display
version: 2.2.1
author: OpenAVC
description: >-
  Controls ViewSonic CDE-series commercial displays (and other ViewSonic
  large-format displays publishing the LFD RS-232 & LAN protocol) on TCP
  port 5000 or direct RS-232. Power, input select, volume, mute,
  brightness, backlight, picture values, freeze and screen blank, PIP,
  video-wall tiling, keypad navigation, control locks, the display's
  clock, ambient sensor readings, and a Wake-on-LAN wake for displays
  whose standby closes the network port. Listens for the display's own
  status pushes and polls for the rest.
source_url: https://viewsonicvsa.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/43000470279-viewsonic-lfd-rs-232-lan-protocol
tags: [display, viewsonic, cde, cde31, signage, lfd, rs232, lan-control]
verified: true
simulated: true
ports: [5000]
min_platform_version: "0.37.0"
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: ViewSonic
    models:
      - CDE8631
      - CDE8631-1C
      - CDE8631-1B
    confidence: full
    notes: >-
      Verified on a CDE8631-1C over TCP 5000, on firmware 20250609234259
      and 20260526125505. Set Power Save to Sleep in the Advanced menu for
      network control in standby and Power On; in Hibernate the display
      leaves the network, and it does not wake over Wake-on-LAN.
      Inputs: HDMI 1-3, USB-C, the embedded Android player, and the slot-in
      PC when one is installed. PIP, surround sound and the customized hot
      keys are not available on this model. Brightness, contrast and
      sharpness are refused while the embedded Android source is showing;
      use Backlight there.
  - manufacturer: ViewSonic
    models:
      - CDE4331
      - CDE5531
      - CDE6531
      - CDE7531
      - CDE9831
      - CDE4331-1C
      - CDE5531-1C
      - CDE6531-1C
      - CDE7531-1C
      - CDE9831-1C
      - CDE4331-1B
      - CDE5531-1B
      - CDE6531-1B
      - CDE7531-1B
      - CDE9831-1B
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      The rest of the CDE31 series, documented in the same user guide as the
      verified CDE8631-1C. The slot-in PC input exists on 65-inch models and
      larger.
  - manufacturer: ViewSonic
    models:
      - CDE4330
      - CDE5530
      - CDE6530
      - CDE7530
      - CDE8630
      - CDE9830
      - CDE4320
      - CDE5520
      - CDE6520
      - CDE7520
      - CDE8620
      - CDE7512
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      The CDE30 and CDE20 series publish this protocol in their per-model
      RS-232 documentation. Every ViewSonic large-format display on the
      brand-wide LFD RS-232 & LAN protocol, including ViewBoard IFP
      interactive displays and NMP media players, is expected to work.
      Function support varies by model (PIP and tiling are
      model-dependent); an unsupported command answers a reject the driver
      ignores.
protocols: [viewsonic_lfd_rs232_lan]
transport: tcp
transports: [tcp, serial]
delimiter: "\r"
command_prefix: "8{monitor_id:02d}"
command_suffix: "\r"

help:
  overview: >-
    ViewSonic commercial displays speaking the LFD RS-232 & LAN protocol.
    Power, input, audio, picture controls, screen blank and freeze, PIP,
    video-wall tiling, and control locks. The display pushes power / input
    / brightness / backlight / volume / mute changes on its own; everything
    else is polled every 30 seconds.
  setup: >-
    1. Network control: connect the display's LAN port, note the IP address
       from the network settings, and add it here (TCP port 5000, no
       login). Monitor ID stays 1 for LAN control. On the CDE31 series,
       LAN Control must be On in the Advanced section of the display's
       on-screen menu.

    2. Standby: on the CDE31 series set Power Save in the Advanced menu to
       Sleep. In Sleep the display keeps answering in standby, and Power On
       turns it on in about 3 seconds. In Hibernate it leaves the network
       about 6 seconds after Power Off and has to be turned on with its
       remote or power key. On other models, check the standby or
       network-standby setting the same way, or use Wake (Wake-on-LAN) for
       a model whose standby turns the network off (turn WOL on in the
       display's settings; the MAC address is learned on every connection).

    3. Serial control: connect the display's RS-232 port (9-pin male) with a
       null-modem cable, or a straight cable plus the null-modem adapter
       that comes with the display, at 9600 8N1 (fixed). On the CDE31
       series turn RS232 On in the Advanced menu. On a daisy chain, set
       each display's Monitor ID in its menu and match it here.
  connection: >-
    A display whose standby mode shuts the network down (Hibernate on the
    CDE31 series) does not answer on TCP 5000 until it is turned on at the
    display. On models that wake over the network, Wake (Wake-on-LAN)
    turns it on; the display's MAC is learned automatically on every
    connection, or can be typed in under Edit Device for a display that has
    never connected.

discovery:
  # Active fingerprint: the documented communication-link test (Get-ACK)
  # on TCP 5000 answers the framed reply '801rz000', protocol-unique
  # grammar. A display in network-dead standby will not probe; the OUI /
  # alias hints still surface it.
  tcp_probe:
    port: 5000
    send_ascii: "801gz000\r"
    expect: "01rz"
  oui:
    - "00:0b:14"
    - "04:0e:c2"
  manufacturer_alias:
    - viewsonic
    - viewsonic corporation

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 5000
  monitor_id: 1
  poll_interval: 30
  inter_command_delay: 0.05
  baudrate: 9600
  parity: "N"
  bytesize: 8
  stopbits: 1
  mac_address: ""
  ambient_sensors: false

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
    description: Display IP address. LAN control uses TCP port 5000.
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 5000
    label: TCP Port
    description: Default 5000 for ViewSonic LAN control (fixed on the display).
  monitor_id:
    type: integer
    default: 1
    min: 1
    max: 98
    label: Monitor ID
    description: Leave at 1 for LAN control. RS-232 daisy chains address IDs 1-98 (set per display in the OSD).
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 30
    min: 5
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.05
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
  baudrate:
    type: integer
    default: 9600
    label: Baud Rate (serial)
    description: ViewSonic LFD serial control is fixed at 9600 8N1.
  mac_address:
    type: string
    default: ""
    label: MAC Address (for Wake-on-LAN)
    learned_from: mac_address
    description: Filled in from the display when it connects, so Wake (Wake-on-LAN) can still reach it after a restart while it is off. Fill it in yourself only for a display that has never connected.
  ambient_sensors:
    type: boolean
    default: false
    label: Read Ambient Sensors
    description: Reads ambient temperature, humidity, light and presence on every poll, from the display's own sensors or a ViewSonic Smart Hub accessory. Each reading holds the display's replies for about 2 seconds, so leave it off unless you use these values.

state_variables:
  power:
    type: enum
    values: ["on", standby]
    label: Power
    control: true
    help: "on = picture up; standby = off. Whether the display keeps answering in standby depends on its power-save setting (Sleep on the CDE31 series)."
  source:
    type: string
    label: Source
    control: true
    help: "Active input (hdmi1-4, dp1/dp2, typec1/typec2, ops, android, vga1-3, dvi, internal, ypbpr, av, svideo, tv). A code this driver does not know reads back as its two-character suffix."
  signal_detected:
    type: boolean
    label: Signal Present
    help: Whether the current input has a detected video signal (reported with every input read). After an input change the display reports the signal about 5 seconds later.
  volume:
    type: integer
    label: Volume
    control: true
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Speaker volume, 0-100.
  mute:
    type: boolean
    label: Mute
    control: true
    help: Audio mute state.
  brightness:
    type: integer
    label: Brightness
    control: true
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Picture brightness, 0-100. On Android-based models the display refuses it while the embedded Android source is showing; Backlight sets the level there.
  backlight:
    type: integer
    label: Backlight
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Backlight level, 0-100. On Android-based models this is the level for the embedded Android source.
  contrast:
    type: integer
    label: Contrast
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Picture contrast, 0-100.
  sharpness:
    type: integer
    label: Sharpness
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Picture sharpness, 0-100.
  color:
    type: integer
    label: Color
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Color saturation, 0-100.
  tint:
    type: integer
    label: Tint
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: Picture tint, 0-100.
  backlight_on:
    type: boolean
    label: Backlight On
    help: False while the screen is blanked (backlight off) with the display still on and controllable.
  freeze:
    type: boolean
    label: Freeze
    help: Whether the picture is frozen.
  touch_enabled:
    type: boolean
    label: Touch Enabled
    help: Whether the touch screen is enabled (touch-capable models only).
  power_lock:
    type: enum
    values: [locked, unlocked]
    label: Power Key Lock
    help: Whether the display's power key is locked. RS-232/LAN power control keeps working while locked.
  button_lock:
    type: enum
    values: [locked, unlocked]
    label: Button Lock
    help: Whether the front-panel buttons and remote are locked (power key excepted).
  menu_lock:
    type: enum
    values: [locked, unlocked]
    label: Menu Lock
    help: Whether the OSD menu key is locked.
  remote_control_mode:
    type: enum
    values: [disabled, enabled, passthrough]
    label: Remote Control Mode
    help: "IR remote handling: enabled, disabled, or passed through to a device on the RS-232 port."
  pip_mode:
    type: enum
    values: ["off", pip, pbp]
    label: PIP Mode
    help: Picture-in-picture / picture-by-picture mode (model-dependent).
  pip_input:
    type: string
    label: PIP Input
    help: Source shown in the PIP window (model-dependent).
  tiling_mode:
    type: boolean
    label: Tiling Mode
    help: Video-wall tiling on/off (video-wall models only).
  tiling_compensation:
    type: boolean
    label: Tiling Bezel Compensation
    help: Video-wall bezel-width compensation on/off.
  tiling_layout:
    type: string
    label: Tiling Layout
    help: Video-wall layout as <horizontal>x<vertical> monitors (e.g. 3x3).
  tiling_position:
    type: integer
    label: Tiling Position
    help: This display's position in the video wall (1-25). Some models report 0 while tiling is off; the CDE31 reports the stored position.
  thermal_c:
    type: integer
    label: Temperature (C)
    help: Internal temperature reported by the display, degrees Celsius.
  amb_temperature_c:
    type: number
    label: Ambient Temperature (C)
    help: Ambient temperature from the display's sensor or a Smart Hub accessory. Read only while Read Ambient Sensors is on.
  amb_humidity:
    type: number
    label: Ambient Humidity (%)
    help: Ambient relative humidity from a Smart Hub accessory (0 on a display without one). Read only while Read Ambient Sensors is on.
  amb_light:
    type: integer
    label: Ambient Light
    unknown: [-1]
    help: Ambient light level from the display's sensor or a Smart Hub accessory. Empty while the display has no light reading. Read only while Read Ambient Sensors is on.
  amb_presence:
    type: boolean
    label: Presence Detected
    help: Presence detected by a Smart Hub accessory's motion sensor, usable as an occupancy trigger. Read only while Read Ambient Sensors is on.
  operation_hours:
    type: integer
    label: Operation Hours
    help: Accumulated operation hours reported by the display.
  device_name:
    type: string
    label: Device Name
    help: Device name reported by the display.
  mac_address:
    type: string
    label: MAC Address
    help: Network MAC address reported by the display (twelve hex digits); Wake (Wake-on-LAN) sends its packet to it.
  ip_address:
    type: string
    label: Reported IP Address
    help: IP address the display reports for itself.
  serial_number:
    type: string
    label: Serial Number
    help: Serial number reported by the display.
  firmware_version:
    type: string
    label: Firmware Version
    help: Firmware version reported by the display.

# Every command below is one 9-byte packet: command_prefix supplies the
# length byte and Monitor ID, the send string is [type][code][value]. A set
# whose value the display does not push carries its matching get as a second
# packet ("\r8{monitor_id:02d}g..."), so the state reads back at once; the
# display answers the get after the set has taken effect.
commands:
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    help: Turns the display on, in about 3 seconds on a CDE31. The display has to be answering in standby, so on the CDE31 series set Power Save to Sleep. For a display whose standby turns its network off, use Wake (Wake-on-LAN).
    send: "s!001"
    sets: { power: "on" }
  wake_on_lan:
    label: Wake (Wake-on-LAN)
    help: Sends a Wake-on-LAN packet to the MAC learned on the last connection (or typed in under Edit Device). Runs while the display is off the network, from a macro, schedule or panel button. For ViewSonic displays whose standby turns the network off and that wake this way; turn WOL on in the display's settings. A CDE31 display does not wake this way; use Power On with Power Save set to Sleep.
    available_offline: true
    udp:
      magic_packet: mac_address
    sets: { power: "on" }
  power_off:
    label: Power Off (Standby)
    help: Puts the display in standby. While a system update is waiting, the display shows an update prompt instead and stays on until someone answers it on screen. Whether the network stays up in standby depends on the display's power-save setting (Sleep keeps it, Hibernate drops it).
    send: "s!000"
    sets: { power: standby }
  set_source:
    label: Select Source
    help: Selects an input. The display refuses an input this model does not have.
    send: "s\"{source}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gj000"
    sets: { source: "{source}" }
    params:
      source:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Source
        values:
          - { value: hdmi1, label: "HDMI 1" }
          - { value: hdmi2, label: "HDMI 2" }
          - { value: hdmi3, label: "HDMI 3" }
          - { value: hdmi4, label: "HDMI 4" }
          - { value: dp1, label: "DisplayPort 1" }
          - { value: dp2, label: "DisplayPort 2" }
          - { value: typec1, label: "USB-C 1" }
          - { value: typec2, label: "USB-C 2" }
          - { value: ops, label: "Slot-in PC (OPS/HDBT)" }
          - { value: android, label: "Embedded Android" }
          - { value: vga1, label: "VGA 1" }
          - { value: vga2, label: "VGA 2" }
          - { value: vga3, label: "VGA 3" }
          - { value: dvi, label: "DVI" }
          - { value: internal, label: "Internal Memory" }
          - { value: ypbpr, label: "YPbPr Component" }
          - { value: av, label: "AV" }
          - { value: svideo, label: "S-Video" }
          - { value: tv, label: "TV Tuner" }
        map:
          hdmi1: "004"
          hdmi2: "014"
          hdmi3: "024"
          hdmi4: "034"
          dp1: "009"
          dp2: "029"
          typec1: "019"
          typec2: "039"
          ops: "007"
          android: "00A"
          vga1: "006"
          vga2: "016"
          vga3: "026"
          dvi: "005"
          internal: "008"
          ypbpr: "003"
          av: "001"
          svideo: "002"
          tv: "000"
  input_cycle:
    label: Cycle Input
    help: Steps to the next input in the display's own input list.
    send: "s\"00Z\r8{monitor_id:02d}gj000"
  set_volume:
    label: Set Volume
    help: Sets the speaker volume.
    send: "s5{level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gf000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Volume (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { volume: "{level}" }
  # The volume steps apply after their ack (a get sent with them reads the
  # old level); the display pushes the new level within about a second.
  volume_up:
    label: Volume Up
    help: Raises the speaker volume one step.
    send: "s5901"
  volume_down:
    label: Volume Down
    help: Lowers the speaker volume one step.
    send: "s5900"
  mute_on:
    label: Mute On
    help: Mutes the speakers and audio output.
    send: "s6001\r8{monitor_id:02d}gg000"
    sets: { mute: true }
  mute_off:
    label: Mute Off
    help: Unmutes the speakers and audio output.
    send: "s6000\r8{monitor_id:02d}gg000"
    sets: { mute: false }
  set_brightness:
    label: Set Brightness
    help: Sets the picture brightness. Refused while the embedded Android source is showing on Android-based models; use Backlight there.
    send: "s${level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gb000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Brightness (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { brightness: "{level}" }
  brightness_up:
    label: Brightness Up
    help: Raises the picture brightness one step.
    send: "s$901\r8{monitor_id:02d}gb000"
  brightness_down:
    label: Brightness Down
    help: Lowers the picture brightness one step.
    send: "s$900\r8{monitor_id:02d}gb000"
  set_contrast:
    label: Set Contrast
    help: Sets the picture contrast. Refused while the embedded Android source is showing on Android-based models.
    send: "s#{level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}ga000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Contrast (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { contrast: "{level}" }
  set_sharpness:
    label: Set Sharpness
    help: Sets the picture sharpness. Refused while the embedded Android source is showing on Android-based models.
    send: "s%{level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gc000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Sharpness (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { sharpness: "{level}" }
  set_color:
    label: Set Color
    help: Sets the picture color saturation.
    send: "s&{level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gd000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Color (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { color: "{level}" }
  set_tint:
    label: Set Tint
    help: Sets the picture tint.
    send: "s'{level:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}ge000"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Tint (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
    sets: { tint: "{level}" }
  backlight_off:
    label: Screen Blank (Backlight Off)
    help: Blanks the picture by switching the backlight off. The display stays on and keeps answering, so it suits a short pause.
    send: "s(000\r8{monitor_id:02d}gh000"
    sets: { backlight_on: false }
  backlight_on:
    label: Screen Unblank (Backlight On)
    help: Switches the backlight back on. The display ignores it for about 2 seconds after a Screen Blank, and the screen then reads as still blank; send it again after that.
    send: "s(001\r8{monitor_id:02d}gh000"
    sets: { backlight_on: true }
  freeze_on:
    label: Freeze Picture
    help: Holds the current picture on screen.
    send: "s*001"
    sets: { freeze: true }
  freeze_off:
    label: Unfreeze Picture
    help: Releases a frozen picture.
    send: "s*000"
    sets: { freeze: false }
  set_color_mode:
    label: Set Color Mode
    help: Sets the color temperature preset. The display does not report the preset back.
    send: "s){mode}"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Color Mode
        values:
          - { value: normal, label: Normal }
          - { value: warm, label: Warm }
          - { value: cold, label: Cold }
          - { value: personal, label: Personal }
        map: { normal: "000", warm: "001", cold: "002", personal: "003" }
  set_picture_size:
    label: Set Picture Size
    help: Sets how the picture fills the screen. The display does not report the size back.
    send: "s1{size}"
    params:
      size:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Picture Size
        values:
          - { value: full, label: "Full (16:9)" }
          - { value: normal, label: "Normal (4:3)" }
          - { value: real, label: "Real (1:1)" }
        map: { full: "000", normal: "001", real: "002" }
  set_bass:
    label: Set Bass
    help: Sets the speaker bass. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s.{level:03d}"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Bass (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
  set_treble:
    label: Set Treble
    help: Sets the speaker treble. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s/{level:03d}"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Treble (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
  set_balance:
    label: Set Balance
    help: Sets the left-right speaker balance; 50 is centered. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s0{level:03d}"
    params:
      level: { type: integer, required: true, label: Balance (0-100), min: 0, max: 100 }
  surround_on:
    label: Surround Sound On
    help: Turns the speakers' surround effect on. Not available on the CDE31 series. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s-001"
  surround_off:
    label: Surround Sound Off
    help: Turns the speakers' surround effect off. Not available on the CDE31 series.
    send: "s-000"
  set_osd_language:
    label: Set OSD Language
    help: Sets the language of the on-screen menus. Some models offer more languages than these three. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s2{language}"
    params:
      language:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Language
        values:
          - { value: english, label: English }
          - { value: french, label: French }
          - { value: spanish, label: Spanish }
        map: { english: "000", french: "001", spanish: "002" }
  set_pip_mode:
    label: Set PIP Mode
    help: Shows a second source in a window (PIP) or side by side (PBP), or turns that off. Not available on the CDE31 series.
    send: "s9{mode}"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: PIP Mode
        values:
          - { value: "off", label: "Off" }
          - { value: pip, label: PIP }
          - { value: pbp, label: PBP }
        map: { "off": "000", pip: "001", pbp: "002" }
    sets: { pip_mode: "{mode}" }
  set_pip_input:
    label: Set PIP Input
    help: Selects the source shown in the PIP window. Not available on the CDE31 series.
    send: "s7{source}"
    params:
      source:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: PIP Source
        values:
          - { value: hdmi1, label: "HDMI 1" }
          - { value: hdmi2, label: "HDMI 2" }
          - { value: hdmi3, label: "HDMI 3" }
          - { value: hdmi4, label: "HDMI 4" }
          - { value: dp1, label: "DisplayPort 1" }
          - { value: dp2, label: "DisplayPort 2" }
          - { value: typec1, label: "USB-C 1" }
          - { value: typec2, label: "USB-C 2" }
          - { value: ops, label: "Slot-in PC (OPS/HDBT)" }
          - { value: android, label: "Embedded Android" }
          - { value: vga1, label: "VGA 1" }
          - { value: vga2, label: "VGA 2" }
          - { value: vga3, label: "VGA 3" }
          - { value: dvi, label: "DVI" }
          - { value: internal, label: "Internal Memory" }
          - { value: ypbpr, label: "YPbPr Component" }
          - { value: av, label: "AV" }
          - { value: svideo, label: "S-Video" }
          - { value: tv, label: "TV Tuner" }
        map:
          hdmi1: "004"
          hdmi2: "014"
          hdmi3: "024"
          hdmi4: "034"
          dp1: "009"
          dp2: "029"
          typec1: "019"
          typec2: "039"
          ops: "007"
          android: "00A"
          vga1: "006"
          vga2: "016"
          vga3: "026"
          dvi: "005"
          internal: "008"
          ypbpr: "003"
          av: "001"
          svideo: "002"
          tv: "000"
  set_pip_sound:
    label: Set PIP Sound
    help: Chooses whether the main picture's or the PIP window's audio plays. Not available on the CDE31 series. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s:{from_window}"
    params:
      from_window:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Sound From
        values:
          - { value: main, label: Main Picture }
          - { value: sub, label: Sub Picture }
        map: { main: "000", sub: "001" }
  set_pip_position:
    label: Set PIP Position
    help: Moves the PIP window. Not available on the CDE31 series. The display does not report it back.
    send: "s;{position}"
    params:
      position:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Position
        values:
          - { value: up, label: Up }
          - { value: down, label: Down }
          - { value: left, label: Left }
          - { value: right, label: Right }
        map: { up: "000", down: "001", left: "002", right: "003" }
  set_tiling_mode:
    label: Set Tiling Mode
    help: Turns video-wall tiling on or off. With tiling on, the display shows its own part of the picture for its position in the wall.
    send: "sP{mode}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gv000"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Tiling
        values:
          - { value: "on", label: "On" }
          - { value: "off", label: "Off" }
        map: { "on": "001", "off": "000" }
  set_tiling_compensation:
    label: Set Tiling Bezel Compensation
    help: Turns compensation for the bezels between video-wall displays on or off.
    send: "sQ{mode}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gw000"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Bezel Compensation
        values:
          - { value: "on", label: "On" }
          - { value: "off", label: "Off" }
        map: { "on": "001", "off": "000" }
  set_tiling_layout:
    label: Set Tiling Layout
    help: Sets the video-wall size in displays, 1-9 across and 1-9 down.
    send: "sR0{horizontal}{vertical}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gx000"
    params:
      horizontal: { type: integer, required: true, label: Horizontal Monitors (1-9), min: 1, max: 9 }
      vertical: { type: integer, required: true, label: Vertical Monitors (1-9), min: 1, max: 9 }
  set_tiling_position:
    label: Set Tiling Position
    help: Sets this display's position in the video wall, counted across from the top left (1-25).
    send: "sS{position:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gy000"
    params:
      position: { type: integer, required: true, label: Position (1-25), min: 1, max: 25 }
    sets: { tiling_position: "{position}" }
  nav_key:
    label: Press Navigation Key
    help: Presses a key on the display's keypad, for moving through its on-screen menus.
    send: "sA{key}"
    params:
      key:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Key
        values:
          - { value: up, label: Up }
          - { value: down, label: Down }
          - { value: left, label: Left }
          - { value: right, label: Right }
          - { value: enter, label: Enter }
          - { value: input, label: Input }
          - { value: menu, label: Menu }
          - { value: exit, label: Exit }
        map: { up: "000", down: "001", left: "002", right: "003", enter: "004", input: "005", menu: "006", exit: "007" }
  press_number:
    label: Press Number Key
    help: Presses a number key, as on the remote.
    send: "s@{number:03d}"
    params:
      number: { type: integer, required: true, label: Number (0-9), min: 0, max: 9 }
  custom_hot_key:
    label: Trigger Custom Hot Key
    help: Runs one of the model's customized hot keys (1 opens the MVBA app on models that have it). Not available on the CDE31 series.
    send: "sX{key:03d}"
    params:
      key: { type: integer, required: true, label: Hot Key (1-999), min: 1, max: 999 }
  set_clock:
    label: Set Clock
    help: Sets the display's own date and time, which its built-in on and off schedules use.
    send: "sVY{year:02d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}sVM{month:02d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}sVD{day:02d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}sWH{hour:02d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}sWM{minute:02d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}sWS{second:02d}"
    params:
      year: { type: integer, required: true, label: Year, min: 2017, max: 2098, offset: 2000 }
      month: { type: integer, required: true, label: Month (1-12), min: 1, max: 12 }
      day: { type: integer, required: true, label: Day (1-31), min: 1, max: 31 }
      hour: { type: integer, required: true, label: Hour (0-23), min: 0, max: 23 }
      minute: { type: integer, required: true, label: Minute (0-59), min: 0, max: 59 }
      second: { type: integer, required: true, label: Second (0-59), min: 0, max: 59 }
  restore_default:
    label: Restore Factory Defaults
    confirm: "Returns the display to its factory settings, erasing its own configuration."
    help: Returns the display to its factory settings, erasing its own configuration.
    send: "s~000"
  raw_command:
    label: Send Raw Command
    help: "Send any set/get from the LFD protocol tables: pick the type ('A'/'a' are the backlight-level pair), give the one-character command code and the three-character value field (e.g. type s, code $, value 076)."
    send: "{cmd_type}{code}{value}"
    params:
      cmd_type:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Type
        values:
          - { value: s, label: Set }
          - { value: g, label: Get }
          - { value: A, label: Set (backlight pair) }
          - { value: a, label: Get (backlight pair) }
      code:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Command Code
        pattern: '^[\x20-\x7e]$'
      value:
        type: string
        required: true
        default: "000"
        label: Value
        pattern: '^[0-9A-Za-z]{3}$'

# Settings are written as a full frame (command_prefix does not apply to a
# setting write) followed by the matching get, so the value reads back at
# once. Enum settings take the state variable's own tokens and write the
# display's code through map:.
device_settings:
  backlight:
    type: integer
    min: 0
    max: 100
    label: Backlight
    help: Backlight level, 0-100. The display keeps it.
    state_key: backlight
    default: 100
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}AB{value:03d}\r8{monitor_id:02d}aB000\r"
  power_lock:
    type: enum
    values:
      - { value: unlocked, label: Unlocked }
      - { value: locked, label: Locked }
    map: { unlocked: "000", locked: "001" }
    label: Power Key Lock
    help: Locks the power key on the display and the remote. Power control over the network and RS-232 keeps working.
    state_key: power_lock
    default: unlocked
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}s4{value}\r8{monitor_id:02d}go000\r"
  button_lock:
    type: enum
    values:
      - { value: unlocked, label: Unlocked }
      - { value: locked, label: Locked }
    map: { unlocked: "000", locked: "001" }
    label: Button Lock
    help: Locks the display's buttons and the remote, except the power key.
    state_key: button_lock
    default: unlocked
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}s8{value}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gp000\r"
  menu_lock:
    type: enum
    values:
      - { value: unlocked, label: Unlocked }
      - { value: locked, label: Locked }
    map: { unlocked: "000", locked: "001" }
    label: Menu Lock
    help: Locks the menu key, so the on-screen menu cannot be opened from the display or the remote.
    state_key: menu_lock
    default: unlocked
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}s>{value}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gq000\r"
  remote_control_mode:
    type: enum
    values:
      - { value: enabled, label: Enabled }
      - { value: disabled, label: Disabled }
      - { value: passthrough, label: Pass Through }
    map: { enabled: "001", disabled: "000", passthrough: "002" }
    label: Remote Control Mode
    help: Enabled lets the IR remote control the display. Disabled ignores it. Pass Through sends the remote's keys out of the RS-232 port instead of acting on them. Changing from Pass Through to Enabled, a CDE31 can report Disabled for a moment; the next poll shows Enabled.
    state_key: remote_control_mode
    default: enabled
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}sB{value}\r8{monitor_id:02d}gn000\r"
  touch:
    type: boolean
    label: Touch Screen
    help: Turns the touch screen on or off (touch-capable models only).
    state_key: touch_enabled
    default: true
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "8{monitor_id:02d}s={value:d}03\r8{monitor_id:02d}g=003\r"

quick_actions: [power_on, power_off, mute_on, mute_off]

# Replies are CR-framed and matched after the frame is stripped of the CR
# and any trailing spaces; the length byte is any character and the Monitor
# ID must be this display's. NUL padding survives the strip, so the info
# rules end in \x00*. First match wins.
responses:
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rl(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: power, map: { "001": "on", "000": standby } }
  # Get-Input packs two facts: digit 1 = signal detected, digits 2-3 = the
  # source code's last two characters (every documented code has a unique
  # suffix).
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rj([01])(\w\w)$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: signal_detected, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }
      - group: 2
        state: source
        map:
          "04": hdmi1
          "14": hdmi2
          "24": hdmi3
          "34": hdmi4
          "09": dp1
          "29": dp2
          "19": typec1
          "39": typec2
          "07": ops
          "0A": android
          "06": vga1
          "16": vga2
          "26": vga3
          "05": dvi
          "08": internal
          "03": ypbpr
          "01": av
          "02": svideo
          "00": tv
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}ru.(\w\w)$'
    mappings:
      - group: 1
        state: pip_input
        map:
          "04": hdmi1
          "14": hdmi2
          "24": hdmi3
          "34": hdmi4
          "09": dp1
          "29": dp2
          "19": typec1
          "39": typec2
          "07": ops
          "0A": android
          "06": vga1
          "16": vga2
          "26": vga3
          "05": dvi
          "08": internal
          "03": ypbpr
          "01": av
          "02": svideo
          "00": tv
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rf(\d{3})$'
    set: { volume: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rb(\d{3})$'
    set: { brightness: "$1" }
  # Backlight level: its own get type 'a', code 'B'; reply assumed r + B.
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rB(\d{3})$'
    set: { backlight: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}ra(\d{3})$'
    set: { contrast: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rc(\d{3})$'
    set: { sharpness: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rd(\d{3})$'
    set: { color: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}re(\d{3})$'
    set: { tint: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rg(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: mute, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rh(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: backlight_on, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}ri(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: freeze, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  # Locks are not inverted on this protocol: 000 unlock / 001 lock.
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}ro(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: power_lock, map: { "001": locked, "000": unlocked } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rp(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: button_lock, map: { "001": locked, "000": unlocked } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rq(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: menu_lock, map: { "001": locked, "000": unlocked } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rn(00[012])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: remote_control_mode, map: { "000": disabled, "001": enabled, "002": passthrough } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rt(00[012])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: pip_mode, map: { "000": "off", "001": pip, "002": pbp } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rv(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: tiling_mode, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rw(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: tiling_compensation, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  # Tiling H x V arrives as "0" + H digit + V digit; the layout string is
  # the pair spelled out.
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}rx0([1-9][1-9])$'
    mappings:
      - group: 1
        state: tiling_layout
        map:
          "11": "1x1"
          "12": "1x2"
          "13": "1x3"
          "14": "1x4"
          "15": "1x5"
          "16": "1x6"
          "17": "1x7"
          "18": "1x8"
          "19": "1x9"
          "21": "2x1"
          "22": "2x2"
          "23": "2x3"
          "24": "2x4"
          "25": "2x5"
          "26": "2x6"
          "27": "2x7"
          "28": "2x8"
          "29": "2x9"
          "31": "3x1"
          "32": "3x2"
          "33": "3x3"
          "34": "3x4"
          "35": "3x5"
          "36": "3x6"
          "37": "3x7"
          "38": "3x8"
          "39": "3x9"
          "41": "4x1"
          "42": "4x2"
          "43": "4x3"
          "44": "4x4"
          "45": "4x5"
          "46": "4x6"
          "47": "4x7"
          "48": "4x8"
          "49": "4x9"
          "51": "5x1"
          "52": "5x2"
          "53": "5x3"
          "54": "5x4"
          "55": "5x5"
          "56": "5x6"
          "57": "5x7"
          "58": "5x8"
          "59": "5x9"
          "61": "6x1"
          "62": "6x2"
          "63": "6x3"
          "64": "6x4"
          "65": "6x5"
          "66": "6x6"
          "67": "6x7"
          "68": "6x8"
          "69": "6x9"
          "71": "7x1"
          "72": "7x2"
          "73": "7x3"
          "74": "7x4"
          "75": "7x5"
          "76": "7x6"
          "77": "7x7"
          "78": "7x8"
          "79": "7x9"
          "81": "8x1"
          "82": "8x2"
          "83": "8x3"
          "84": "8x4"
          "85": "8x5"
          "86": "8x6"
          "87": "8x7"
          "88": "8x8"
          "89": "8x9"
          "91": "9x1"
          "92": "9x2"
          "93": "9x3"
          "94": "9x4"
          "95": "9x5"
          "96": "9x6"
          "97": "9x7"
          "98": "9x8"
          "99": "9x9"
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}ry(\d{3})$'
    set: { tiling_position: "$1" }
  # Thermal: 3 chars, below zero as "-NN".
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r0(-?\d+)$'
    set: { thermal_c: "$1" }
  # Function On_Off get: [1/0 on/off][function id 01 backlight / 02 freeze / 03 touch].
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r=([01])01$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: backlight_on, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r=([01])02$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: freeze, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r=([01])03$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: touch_enabled, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }
  # 32-byte info replies: value, then padding (NULs per the spec; the
  # CDE31's spaces are gone by the time a rule sees the frame).
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r1(\d+)\x00*$'
    set: { operation_hours: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r4([^\x00]+)\x00*$'
    set: { device_name: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r5([0-9A-Fa-f]{12})\x00*$'
    set: { mac_address: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r6([^\x00]+)\x00*$'
    set: { ip_address: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r7([^\x00]+)\x00*$'
    set: { serial_number: "$1" }
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r8([^\x00]+)\x00*$'
    set: { firmware_version: "$1" }
  # Smart Hub: a marker letter and a number per sensor, any subset in any
  # order. The spec gives fixed 6-byte fields (A-05.0B030.0C00080D00001); a
  # CDE31 sends them unpadded (A28.2B000.0C-1D00000), with C-1 for no light
  # reading, and "none" for a single-field get without the accessory. Each
  # field is an optional lookahead, so one rule reads whatever arrives and a
  # field that is missing leaves its variable alone.
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}r:(?=(?:.*?A(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?))?)(?=(?:.*?B(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?))?)(?=(?:.*?C(-?\d+))?)(?=(?:.*?D(\d+))?)'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: amb_temperature_c }
      - { group: 2, state: amb_humidity }
      - { group: 3, state: amb_light }
      - { group: 4, state: amb_presence, map: { "00001": "true", "00000": "false", "1": "true", "0": "false" } }
  # A set's acknowledgement ('+'). Last, so every reply above is tried
  # first; a refusal ('-') is left unmatched, so it still shows as one.
  - match: '^.{monitor_id:02d}\+$'
    set: {}

# Identity (and the MAC the Wake-on-LAN command needs) once per connection.
on_connect:
  - "8{monitor_id:02d}g4000\r"
  - "8{monitor_id:02d}g5000\r"
  - "8{monitor_id:02d}g6000\r"
  - "8{monitor_id:02d}g7000\r"
  - "8{monitor_id:02d}g8000\r"

# The poll cycle: every get with a state variable behind it. A model that
# lacks a function answers '-', which matches no rule.
polling:
  queries:
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gl000\r", query_for: power }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gj000\r", query_for: source }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gf000\r", query_for: volume }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gg000\r", query_for: mute }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gb000\r", query_for: brightness }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}aB000\r", query_for: backlight }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}ga000\r", query_for: contrast }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gc000\r", query_for: sharpness }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gd000\r", query_for: color }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}ge000\r", query_for: tint }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gh000\r", query_for: backlight_on }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gi000\r", query_for: freeze }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}g=003\r", query_for: touch_enabled }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}go000\r", query_for: power_lock }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gp000\r", query_for: button_lock }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gq000\r", query_for: menu_lock }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gn000\r", query_for: remote_control_mode }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}g0000\r", query_for: thermal_c }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gt000\r", query_for: pip_mode }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gu000\r", query_for: pip_input }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gv000\r", query_for: tiling_mode }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gw000\r", query_for: tiling_compensation }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gx000\r", query_for: tiling_layout }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gy000\r", query_for: tiling_position }
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}g1000\r", query_for: operation_hours }
    # Last: the display holds every reply for about 2 s while it reads the
    # ambient sensors, so this runs only when that option is on.
    - { send: "8{monitor_id:02d}g:000\r", when: ambient_sensors, query_for: amb_temperature_c }

# Over RS-232 nothing else notices a display that stops answering. Get-ACK
# is the protocol's own link test, answered on and in standby; a refusal
# ('-') counts as an answer too.
liveness:
  send: "8{monitor_id:02d}gz000\r"
  expect: '\d\d(?:rz|-)'
  interval: 30
  timeout: 5
  max_failures: 2

# ── Simulator ──
# Models the CDE8631-1C the driver was verified on. One dispatcher script
# handler speaks the whole framed grammar: sets answer '+' / '-', gets the
# framed 'r' reply, info gets the 32-byte form padded with spaces, and a
# packet for another Monitor ID gets no reply (ID 99 is answered as display
# 1, as the spec says). Everything answers in standby, as it does in the
# display's Sleep power-save mode. What that model refuses is refused here:
# PIP, surround, the hot keys, the slot-in PC with no module installed,
# inputs it does not have, and brightness while the Android source shows.
# The notifications map sends the auto-reply frames on every change, from a
# command or from the Simulator UI; its frames carry Monitor ID 01, the ID a
# display answers on the LAN. State is kept in the driver's own vocabulary so
# the Simulator UI's controls read and write it directly, and a magic packet
# from Wake (Wake-on-LAN) applies its declared sets: the simulated display
# comes on.
# Not modelled: the about 5 s delay before a real display pushes an input
# change, the about 2 s it holds every reply while it reads its sensors,
# and the Screen Unblank it ignores within about 2 s of a Screen Blank.
simulator:
  initial_state:
    power: "on"
    source: hdmi1
    signal_detected: true
    volume: 30
    mute: false
    brightness: 50
    backlight: 80
    contrast: 50
    sharpness: 50
    color: 50
    tint: 50
    backlight_on: true
    freeze: false
    touch_enabled: true
    power_lock: unlocked
    button_lock: unlocked
    menu_lock: unlocked
    remote_control_mode: enabled
    tiling_mode: false
    tiling_compensation: false
    tiling_layout: 1x1
    tiling_position: 1
    thermal_c: 48
    amb_temperature_c: 23.5
    amb_humidity: 45.0
    amb_light: 80
    amb_presence: true
    operation_hours: 33
    device_name: CDE8631-1C
    mac_address: 00e04c123456
    ip_address: 192.168.1.50
    serial_number: Y9125ABC0001
    firmware_version: "20250609234259"
  delays:
    command_response: 0.02
  notifications:
    power: { "on": "801rl001", standby: "801rl000" }
    source:
      hdmi1: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}04"
      hdmi2: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}14"
      hdmi3: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}24"
      hdmi4: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}34"
      dp1: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}09"
      dp2: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}29"
      typec1: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}19"
      typec2: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}39"
      ops: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}07"
      android: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}0A"
      vga1: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}06"
      vga2: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}16"
      vga3: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}26"
      dvi: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}05"
      internal: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}08"
      ypbpr: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}03"
      av: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}01"
      svideo: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}02"
      tv: "801rj{state.signal_detected:d}00"
    volume: "801rf{value:03d}"
    mute: { "true": "801rg001", "false": "801rg000" }
    brightness: "801rb{value:03d}"
    backlight: "801rB{value:03d}"
    backlight_on: { "true": "801r=101", "false": "801r=001" }
    freeze: { "true": "801r=102", "false": "801r=002" }
  command_handlers:
    - match: '.(\d\d)([sgAa])(.)(.*)'
      handler: |
        def _run():
            mid = int(config.get("monitor_id", 1) or 1)
            target = int(match.group(1))
            if target != mid and not (target == 99 and mid == 1):
                return  # another display on the chain: silence
            ftype, code, value = match.group(2), match.group(3), match.group(4)
            head = "8" + format(mid, "02d")

            INPUTS = {"000": "tv", "001": "av", "002": "svideo", "003": "ypbpr",
                      "004": "hdmi1", "014": "hdmi2", "024": "hdmi3", "034": "hdmi4",
                      "005": "dvi", "006": "vga1", "016": "vga2", "026": "vga3",
                      "007": "ops", "008": "internal", "009": "dp1", "029": "dp2",
                      "019": "typec1", "039": "typec2", "00A": "android"}
            CODES = dict((v, k) for k, v in INPUTS.items())
            # The inputs a CDE8631-1C with no slot-in PC accepts, in the
            # order its input cycle steps through them.
            HAS = ["hdmi1", "hdmi2", "hdmi3", "typec1", "android"]
            NUM_SET = {"#": "contrast", "$": "brightness", "%": "sharpness",
                       "&": "color", "'": "tint", "5": "volume"}
            NUM_GET = {"a": "contrast", "b": "brightness", "c": "sharpness",
                       "d": "color", "e": "tint", "f": "volume"}
            BOOL_SET = {"6": "mute", "(": "backlight_on", "*": "freeze",
                        "P": "tiling_mode", "Q": "tiling_compensation"}
            BOOL_GET = {"g": "mute", "h": "backlight_on", "i": "freeze",
                        "v": "tiling_mode", "w": "tiling_compensation"}
            LOCK_SET = {"4": "power_lock", "8": "button_lock", ">": "menu_lock"}
            LOCK_GET = {"o": "power_lock", "p": "button_lock", "q": "menu_lock"}
            RCU = {"000": "disabled", "001": "enabled", "002": "passthrough"}
            FUNC = {"01": "backlight_on", "02": "freeze", "03": "touch_enabled"}
            # Picture sets the embedded Android source refuses (brightness,
            # contrast, sharpness); the sharpness get is refused there too.
            ANDROID_REFUSES = ("$", "#", "%")
            # Set-only codes and how many values each takes (000..n-1).
            ACK_ONLY = {")": 4, "1": 3, "2": 3, "A": 8, "@": 10}
            CLOCK = {"V": {"Y": (17, 98), "M": (1, 12), "D": (1, 31)},
                     "W": {"H": (0, 23), "M": (0, 59), "S": (0, 59)}}
            CLOCK_NOW = {"2": {"Y": "26", "M": "10", "D": "09"},
                         "3": {"H": "12", "M": "00", "S": "00"}}

            def ack(ok):
                respond("4" + format(mid, "02d") + ("+" if ok else "-") + "\r")

            def reply(c, v):
                respond(head + "r" + c + v + "\r")

            def reply32(c, v):
                body = str(v)[:26]
                respond("2" + format(mid, "02d") + "r" + c + body + " " * (26 - len(body)) + "\r")

            def level(v):
                return v.isdigit() and 0 <= int(v) <= 100

            def inv(d, v):
                for k in d:
                    if d[k] == v:
                        return k
                return None

            def hub_field(f):
                if f == "A":
                    return "A" + format(float(state.get("amb_temperature_c") or 0), ".1f")
                if f == "B":
                    return "B" + format(float(state.get("amb_humidity") or 0), "05.1f")
                if f == "C":
                    light = state.get("amb_light")
                    return "C" + ("-1" if light is None else str(int(light)))
                return "D" + ("00001" if state.get("amb_presence") else "00000")

            if ftype == "A":
                if code == "B" and level(value):
                    state["backlight"] = int(value)
                    ack(True)
                else:
                    ack(False)
                return
            if ftype == "a":
                if code == "B":
                    reply("B", format(int(state.get("backlight", 0)), "03d"))
                else:
                    ack(False)
                return

            if ftype == "s":
                if code == "!":
                    if value in ("000", "001"):
                        state["power"] = "on" if value == "001" else "standby"
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code == '"':
                    if value == "00Z":
                        cur = state.get("source")
                        nxt = HAS[(HAS.index(cur) + 1) % len(HAS)] if cur in HAS else HAS[0]
                        state["source"] = nxt
                        ack(True)
                    elif INPUTS.get(value) in HAS:
                        if INPUTS[value] == "android":
                            state["signal_detected"] = True
                        state["source"] = INPUTS[value]
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code in ("$", "5") and value in ("900", "901"):
                    key = "brightness" if code == "$" else "volume"
                    if key == "brightness" and state.get("source") == "android":
                        ack(False)
                        return
                    step = 1 if value == "901" else -1
                    state[key] = max(0, min(100, int(state.get(key, 0)) + step))
                    ack(True)
                elif code in NUM_SET:
                    if code in ANDROID_REFUSES and state.get("source") == "android":
                        ack(False)
                    elif level(value):
                        state[NUM_SET[code]] = int(value)
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code in (".", "/", "0"):
                    ack(level(value))
                elif code in BOOL_SET:
                    if value in ("000", "001"):
                        state[BOOL_SET[code]] = value == "001"
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code in LOCK_SET:
                    if value in ("000", "001"):
                        state[LOCK_SET[code]] = "locked" if value == "001" else "unlocked"
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code == "B":
                    if value in RCU:
                        state["remote_control_mode"] = RCU[value]
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code == "=":
                    fid = value[1:3] if len(value) == 3 else ""
                    if fid in FUNC and value[0] in ("0", "1"):
                        state[FUNC[fid]] = value[0] == "1"
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code == "R":
                    if len(value) == 3 and value[0] == "0" and value[1] in "123456789" and value[2] in "123456789":
                        state["tiling_layout"] = value[1] + "x" + value[2]
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code == "S":
                    if value.isdigit() and 1 <= int(value) <= 25:
                        state["tiling_position"] = int(value)
                        ack(True)
                    else:
                        ack(False)
                elif code in ACK_ONLY:
                    ack(value.isdigit() and int(value) < ACK_ONLY[code])
                elif code in CLOCK:
                    span = CLOCK[code].get(value[:1])
                    num = value[1:]
                    ack(span is not None and num.isdigit() and span[0] <= int(num) <= span[1])
                elif code == "~":
                    ack(value == "000")
                else:
                    # surround, PIP, the hot keys and unknown codes
                    ack(False)
                return

            # ftype == "g"
            if code == "z":
                reply("z", "000")
            elif code == "l":
                reply("l", "001" if state.get("power") == "on" else "000")
            elif code == "j":
                reply("j", ("1" if state.get("signal_detected") else "0") + CODES.get(state.get("source"), "004")[1:])
            elif code == "c" and state.get("source") == "android":
                ack(False)
            elif code in NUM_GET:
                reply(code, format(int(state.get(NUM_GET[code], 0)), "03d"))
            elif code in BOOL_GET:
                reply(code, "001" if state.get(BOOL_GET[code]) else "000")
            elif code in LOCK_GET:
                reply(code, "001" if state.get(LOCK_GET[code]) == "locked" else "000")
            elif code == "n":
                reply("n", inv(RCU, state.get("remote_control_mode")) or "001")
            elif code == "x":
                lay = str(state.get("tiling_layout", "1x1"))
                reply("x", "0" + lay[0] + lay[-1])
            elif code == "y":
                reply("y", format(int(state.get("tiling_position", 1)), "03d"))
            elif code == "=":
                fid = value[1:3] if len(value) == 3 else ""
                if fid in FUNC:
                    reply("=", ("1" if state.get(FUNC[fid]) else "0") + fid)
                else:
                    ack(False)
            elif code in CLOCK_NOW and value[:1] in CLOCK_NOW[code]:
                reply(code, value[:1] + CLOCK_NOW[code][value[:1]])
            elif code == "0":
                t = int(state.get("thermal_c", 0))
                reply("0", format(t, "03d") if t >= 0 else "-" + format(abs(t), "02d"))
            elif code == "1":
                reply32("1", format(int(state.get("operation_hours", 0)), "06d"))
            elif code == "4":
                reply32("4", state.get("device_name", ""))
            elif code == "5":
                reply32("5", str(state.get("mac_address", "")).replace(":", "").lower())
            elif code == "6":
                reply32("6", state.get("ip_address", ""))
            elif code == "7":
                reply32("7", state.get("serial_number", ""))
            elif code == "8":
                reply32("8", state.get("firmware_version", ""))
            elif code == ":":
                hub = bool(config.get("ambient_sensors"))
                if value == "000":
                    if hub:
                        reply32(":", "".join(hub_field(f) for f in "ABCD"))
                    else:
                        reply32(":", "A28.2B000.0C-1D00000")
                elif value in ("00A", "00B", "00C", "00D"):
                    reply32(":", hub_field(value[2]) if hub else "none")
                else:
                    ack(False)
            else:
                # PIP mode / input and unknown codes
                ack(False)

        _run()
  error_modes:
    communication_timeout:
      description: Display stops answering
      behavior: no_response
    garbled_response:
      description: Corrupted data on the wire
      behavior: corrupt_response
